gin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =6 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =6-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5415a0e0865d4a3aa4aa1a816a541670e7be32494c072c6704b45b01fbe0e379"} {"question" "q-142480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79353" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a $7\\times 9$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{rank}(A)=7$ then $\\text{nullity}(A)=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79353" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 7+\\text{nullity}(A) & =9 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =9-7 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79353" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5415a0e0865d4a3aa4aa1a816a541670e7be32494c072c6704b45b01fbe0e379"} {"question" "q-79353" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79353" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79353" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-141230" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $A$ be a $5\\times 7$ matrix. If $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},{b}_{4}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-141230" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=7,$ and since $B$ is formed by $4$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =7 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =7-4 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-141230" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5415a0e0865d4a3aa4aa1a816a541670e7be32494c072c6704b45b01fbe0e379"} {"question" "q-141230" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-141230" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-141230" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-35305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-35305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5,$ and since $B$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-35305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5415a0e0865d4a3aa4aa1a816a541670e7be32494c072c6704b45b01fbe0e379"} {"question" "q-35305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-35305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-35305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5415a0e0865d4a3aa4aa1a816a541670e7be32494c072c6704b45b01fbe0e379"} {"question" "q-142486" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142486" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142486" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-35434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-35434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-35434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5415a0e0865d4a3aa4aa1a816a541670e7be32494c072c6704b45b01fbe0e379"} {"question" "q-35434" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-35434" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-35434" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $A$ be a $6\\times 8$ matrix. If $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},{b}_{4},{b}_{5}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=8,$ and since $B$ is formed by $5$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =8 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =8-5 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5415a0e0865d4a3aa4aa1a816a541670e7be32494c072c6704b45b01fbe0e379"} {"question" "q-142487" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142487" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142487" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}?$ \n\n- $\\text{rank}(A)=2$\n- $A$ is **not** invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $3$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{4}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}=\\text{Span}{\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=4,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(A)=1\\ne 4,$ it follows, by the invertible matrix theorem, that the matrix $A$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=1$ then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is, in fact,  a $1$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5415a0e0865d4a3aa4aa1a816a541670e7be32494c072c6704b45b01fbe0e379"} {"question" "q-79423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-143533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and  $\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}?$ \n\n- $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $det(A)=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-143533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$  \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}=\\text{Span}{\\,[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=2\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible we must have   $det(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-143533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5415a0e0865d4a3aa4aa1a816a541670e7be32494c072c6704b45b01fbe0e379"} {"question" "q-143533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-143533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-143533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79420" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix with only  $1$ pivot column?\n\n- $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- $A$ is not  invertible\n- $\\text{nullity}(A)=3$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79420" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ has just $1$ pivot column, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=4.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem,  $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 1+\\text{nullity}(A) & =4 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79420" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5415a0e0865d4a3aa4aa1a816a541670e7be32494c072c6704b45b01fbe0e379"} {"question" "q-79420" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79420" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79420" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79417" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix which has only $1$ non-pivot column?\n\n- $A$ is  **not** invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{5}.$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79417" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ has $n=5$ columns and $1$ of them is a non-pivot one, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=5-1=4\\ne 5.\n$$\n\n Thus, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is  **not** an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=\\text{rank}(A)=4$,  then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{5}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $n=5$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79417" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5415a0e0865d4a3aa4aa1a816a541670e7be32494c072c6704b45b01fbe0e379"} {"question" "q-79417" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79417" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-79417" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix which has only $3$ non-pivot column?\n\n- $A$ is  **not** invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{7}.$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=7=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ has $n=7$ columns and $3$ of them is a non-pivot one, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=7-3=4\\ne 7.\n$$\n\n Thus,  according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is  **not** an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true.  Indeed, since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=\\text{rank}(A)=4$,  then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{7}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $n=7$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =7 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =7-4 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5415a0e0865d4a3aa4aa1a816a541670e7be32494c072c6704b45b01fbe0e379"} {"question" "q-142544" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142544" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "q-142544" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7d05826fe977738da03bdaa0ba5d961a7c3da9d5dad4fbfa451d7abadb3b68c"} {"question" "e-9963" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a $8 \\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\textrm{rank}(A)=5$ then what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/example-9963.json" "file_sha256" "4fcd712e50f062a31ca9c68953e7db1956d686da2d5b5bfb6b0c1ce52ccd2ea4"} {"question" "e-9963" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then  \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/example-9963.json" "file_sha256" "4fcd712e50f062a31ca9c68953e7db1956d686da2d5b5bfb6b0c1ce52ccd2ea4"} {"question" "e-5425" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $\\mathcal{B}= \\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, find $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/example-5425.json" "file_sha256" "a87123351003354d7bb347dbd7c5f1466ef2fd0521d4c820b7ea966e710be4ed"} {"question" "e-5425" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5,$ and since $\\mathcal{B}$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/example-5425.json" "file_sha256" "a87123351003354d7bb347dbd7c5f1466ef2fd0521d4c820b7ea9